## Two same-class findings mean a missing regression in the owning leaf

A verdict carries a SET of findings, not one finding — see the Review Round
Contract in `.roles/reviewer/role.md`. The repeat rule reads over sets:

- A class repeats when it appears in the finding sets of two CONSECUTIVE
  verdicts on one leaf, not when two single findings match. The sets are
  compared by class; the mechanisms and reproductions inside may differ.
  Class recurrence alone triggers the regression requirement, even when no
  single mechanism repeats across the two sets.
- `repeat-of:` is per finding and mechanism-based, not class-based: every
  finding in the set names its own value — the prior revision and finding id
  this MECHANISM repeats, or `none` when the mechanism is new even inside a
  repeated class. The orchestrator routes on the per-finding field, never on
  a verdict-level one.
- A set holding several classes still produces ONE `to-dev` transition for the
  whole set. The next rework revision answers every finding in the set, and
  adds a named regression test with a narrowing mutant the test kills for each
  repeated class.

When a class repeats, stop routing another rework revision that will be
reviewed from memory. The response is a gate — but the gate lives in the
OWNING leaf: the next rework revision of that same leaf adds the named
regression test for the class, with a narrowing mutant that the test kills,
and the reviewer reviews the revision against that test. This is ordinary
developer rework with the finding as context; it is not a new task.

Worked example: the verdict on revision 4 carries the set {F2, F3} and the
verdict on revision 5 carries {F3}. F3 appears in both consecutive sets, so
F3 repeats as a class, and the revision-6 rework adds the F3 regression test
and mutant however the mechanisms compare. The `repeat-of:` value then splits
by mechanism. Case A: the revision-5 F3 finding repeats the same mechanism as
the revision-4 F3 finding, so it names `repeat-of:` pointing at the
revision-4 F3 finding. Case B: the revision-5 F3 finding carries a different
mechanism in the same F3 class, so the mechanism does not repeat and the
finding names `repeat-of: none` — while the class recurrence still triggers
the regression requirement. F2 appears only in the revision-4 set, so it does
not repeat. Both verdicts route once — revision 4's two findings produce a
single `to-dev`, not one per finding.

A separate task is created only when a checked spec gap says the class cannot
be covered inside the leaf — the `solution-architect` rule 3 `Justified gap`
record: the missing piece, the requirement left incomplete, the sections
checked. That task carries its own budget from
[research workflow](research-workflow.md#research-plan-contract-bounded-by-default)
and is never a research leaf. "The sibling has a harness" or "the class
deserves a general checker" is consistency, not a gap, and is refused by row R1
of [negative evidence](negative-evidence.md#refusal-rows-research-and-validation-budgets).

Evidence for the bound: `TASK-260902-yv0g58` needed a gate after three
same-class revisions. `TASK-260909-2ax2l1` then showed the amplifier: routing
each `repeat-of:` to a NEW generalized gate leaf produced three nested harness
leaves, 42 runs and 14.62 worker hours with zero production bytes changed. The
gate was right; its address was wrong.

## Review fan-out: analysts per row, one verdict run

A review round fans out by surface row instead of asking one run to walk the
whole table: `task-board spawn <LEAF> --role reviewer --review-fanout`
launches one analyst run per surface-row group in parallel at the pinned
reviewer pair, each in a fresh context with one row plus the candidate,
each appending confirmed reproductions to `findings-<row>.json` without
recording a verdict. One verdict run — a plain `task-board spawn <LEAF>
--role reviewer` once the analysts are terminal — then attacks the diff
since the previously reviewed revision first, re-runs every reproduction,
drops what does not reproduce with a note, merges by mechanism, ranks by
severity, spends the remaining budget on a free hunt, and records the
single verdict; its prompt carries the merged analyst draft in a
`Verdict Run Brief`. Only the verdict run handed the revision may
`accept_cr` or `reject_cr`. The analyst and verdict-run brief templates are in
[sub-agent templates](sub-agent-templates.md#review-analyst-fan-out-one-run-per-surface-row)
and
[sub-agent templates](sub-agent-templates.md#review-verdict-run-merge-re-run-one-verdict).

## Loop detector: S1, S2, S3, S4, S5

Every `changes_requested` verdict recording evaluates five loop-detector
signals over the leaf's revision history — the CR revision records plus the
verdict findings arrays stamped beside them, the stored per-revision
candidate patches, and the acceptance snapshots recorded at publication —
and records the outcome on the element as a finding-class note. There are
two recording routes and both evaluate: `reject_cr`, the reviewer-called
mutation, which stamps the verdict findings; and the spawn runtime's
`recordChangeRequestVerdict`, which moves the revision when a reviewer run
completes at `to-dev` without calling `reject_cr` and stamps nothing — the
runtime observes only the verdict branch, so S1 decides on the revision
count and S4 on the stored patches and snapshots there, while S2, S3 and S5
state their bound. What the orchestrator DOES with a fired signal — the
response routing and the spawn gate — is the loop response below. This
section names all five signals, their exact thresholds, the note format,
and the response each recording routes to.

- **S1, revision overrun.** Fires when the leaf's revision count strictly
  exceeds `max(3, 2 x median revisions of the accepted leaves in the same
  Story)`. The median runs over Task/Bug siblings under the same parent whose
  Change Request is accepted or beyond, and doubles exactly: twice the middle
  value for an odd sample, the sum of the two middle values for an even one,
  so no rounding moves the threshold. With fewer than two accepted leaves the
  median is undecidable and the threshold falls back to 3. A count AT the
  threshold does not fire — the contract says "exceed".
- **S2, same class three verdicts running.** Fires when the same finding class
  appears in three consecutive `changes_requested` verdicts ending at the
  just-recorded one. Class identity is the finding `id` — the class slug —
  continued through the `repeat-of` chain: a finding whose `repeat-of` names
  an id present in the immediately previous verdict carries that class
  forward even when its own id was renamed, transitively, so a twice-renamed
  chain still continues its root class. A repeated class with a new mechanism
  legitimately carries `repeat-of: none` and still matches by id. A
  `repeat-of` value that names no id in the previous verdict (free text, a
  misspelling, a dormant class) continues nothing — the class is the id
  alone. A revision that is not `changes_requested`, and a legacy prose
  verdict with unknown classes, break every streak rather than guessing.
- **S3, fix-induced defect.** Fires when a BLOCKING finding of the
  just-recorded verdict carries a `file:line` (or `file:start-end`) anchor
  in its mechanism text that overlaps an INTRODUCED line-span of the same
  file: an added line of the STORED patch of revision N-1 or N-2 whose
  file+content the predecessor patch (N-2 or N-3 respectively) never added.
  The stored `<LEAF>_change-request_rev<N>.patch` resources are cumulative
  base->candidate diffs, so presence in patch(N-1) alone never attributes —
  a hunk from revision 1 sits inside every later patch — and only the
  subtraction decides. Attribution reads only the stored patches, never the
